On Sun, 2019-07-07 at 05:44 -0700, Vaughn Bodden wrote:

problems with cpo, simply is when one datapoint does not make a small
change, but a large one. in that case, then we cannot compute the cpo
values, from the fit with all the data, and then corrected. this as the
correction is to large.

the 'GroupRho' suggest little dependence in time, so the question is if
need the depndence, or if replicate would be enough (replace group=
with replicate=), on the other hand, I'm not sure you want this, or
just a common effect in time.

There is the function

	inla.cpo()

that you can use, that computes 'manually' all cpo's that 'failed' (ie
the assumptions where not satisfied).  But sometimes the problem is
somewhere else.

about setting priors: see the spde book www.r-inla.org/spde-book <http://www.r-inla.org/spde-book>  and
the github version

