Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,

I'm learning r-inla syntax. It is possible to infer the parameters alpha, beta, and gamma for the model defined below? Thanks!

t<-1:100
alpha<-1
beta<-2
gamma<-0.001
delta<-0.7
y1<-rpois(length(t), lambda = alpha * exp(beta+gamma*t))
y2<-arima.sim(list(ar=delta), n = length(t)-1)
y<-y1+y2 + rnorm(length(t))
data<-data.frame(t,y)

I was trying something along the lines of
fit = inla(y ~ f(t, model = 'ar1') + f(t.copy, model = 'exponential', copy=T),
            data=data,
            family = "gaussian",
            control.link=list(model="identity"))
but I'm not sure if that makes sense at all.

From u3eab71733027a24a at groups.google.com  Mon Jul 15 15:57:51 2019
From: u3eab71733027a24a at groups.google.com (Helpdesk)
Date: Mon, 15 Jul 2019 15:57:51 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] structured model definition
Message-ID: <VGh41ze2T9I.ClPhORjOCAAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <VGh41ze2T9I.gGChb7XODwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <VGh41ze2T9I.gGChb7XODwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/VGh41ze2T9I/m/ClPhORjOCAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

with both alpha and beta unknown, you will have problems with the term

	alpha * exp(beta)

with

	y = y1 + y2

and y1 is poisson data, then you're outside the scope of the R-INLA can
do.

H

