On Wednesday, 26 June 2019 10:41:20 UTC+2, James Grecian wrote:
> Hi Everyone,
>
> I am trying to build a space-time log-Gaussian Cox Process model using INLA. I have been basing my code on the great examples given in the SPDE book, particularly Chapters 7 & 8:
> https://becarioprecario.bitbucket.io/spde-gitbook/ch-spacetime.html
>
> However, I am getting stuck when trying to extent this approach in two ways:
> 1. Adding groups to the time mesh.
> 2. Partitioning variance between groups in the spatial field.
>
> My motivation is a dataset of animal migration collected over several years. I would like to explore how environmental covariates influence their distribution over this time.
>
> Currently, I have indexed the time mesh to capture the seasonal migration across all years, so I have four time points (one for each season). The spatial field is then fitted as an AR1 process to allow spring (t = 1) to follow winter (t = 4).
>
> However, I have multiple years of point data and a space-time matched covariate that I would like to include in this model. To do this I need to pass data from each of the 5 years to the matched season in the time mesh, so that each time point has 5 replicates. I don’t have data for all seasons and all years and so I don’t want to fit this as a continuous time model – i.e. from spring in year 1 through to winter in year 5.
>
> The second issue is that the distribution data are from three different populations that follow the same broad spatial pattern, but the population sizes and sampling effort differ. Ideally I would like to partition the spatial field so that these three populations are treated separately.
>
> I have written a reproducible example below that outlines my current approach, borrowing heavily from the SPDE book. If you have any suggestions for how to address this issue, which I think is just a case of indexing, they would be greatly appreciated!
>
> All the best,
>
> James
>
>
> ########################################
> ### Space-time LGCP indexing in INLA ###
> ########################################
>
> # Generate a point process that varies in time and space ###
> # The data are from three populations and five time periods
> # following the SPDE book:
> # https://becarioprecario.bitbucket.io/spde-gitbook/ch-stapp.html
>
> # Load libraries
> require(tidyverse)
> #> Loading required package: tidyverse
> #> Warning: package 'tibble' was built under R version 3.5.2
> #> Warning: package 'dplyr' was built under R version 3.5.2
> #> Warning: package 'stringr' was built under R version 3.5.2
> require(INLA)
> #> Loading required package: INLA
